# ABOUT THE APPLICATION
 
The Term Sheet Extractor reads structured product term sheet PDFs and extracts key financial data into an Excel file. It supports English, French, and German documents. It processes PDFs locally — no data is sent to external servers.
 
# HOW THE APPLICATION WORKS
 
1. The user places PDF files in the input folder (data/input/) or uploads them via the web interface.
2. The tool reads each PDF, detects the language, and extracts financial fields.
3. Results are exported as an Excel file with two sheets:
   - Sheet 1 "Resultats": one row per PDF with all extracted fields.
   - Sheet 2 "Underlying_ISINs": one row per underlying asset with Bloomberg tickers.
4. Optionally, annotated PDFs are generated with coloured highlights on extracted fields.
 
# TERMINAL COMMANDS (for users running via command line)
 
- Full extraction with highlights: python main.py
- Excel only, no highlights (faster): python main.py --no-highlight
- Custom input folder: python main.py --input C:\path\to\pdfs
- Custom output folder: python main.py --output C:\path\to\results
- Detailed logs: python main.py --verbose
- Web interface: streamlit run app.py
 
# EXTRACTED FIELDS — DEFINITIONS
 
PST_ISIN: The 12-character ISIN code identifying the structured product itself. Format: 2 letters (country) + 10 alphanumeric characters. Example: CH1481964653. This is NOT the ISIN of the underlying asset.
 
BIL: Boolean. "True" if the product is issued by Banque Internationale à Luxembourg S.A.
 
CLN: Boolean. "True" if the product is a Credit Linked Note.
 
CAPITAL_PROTECTION: The percentage of the nominal that is guaranteed at maturity. "100" means full capital protection. "0" means no protection (the investor can lose the full investment). When the PDF does not explicitly state this field, the tool infers it from the SSPA product type (see below). Inferred values are reliable but should be verified for edge cases.
 
MATURITY: The final maturity or fixing date, normalised to ISO format YYYY-MM-DD. "Open End" means the product has no fixed expiry.
 
WORST_OR_AVERAGE: Indicates how the product's payoff is linked to its underlyings.
- "W" (Worst-of): the payoff depends on the worst-performing underlying. This is the most common structure for barrier reverse convertibles and autocallables.
- "A" (Average): the payoff depends on the average performance of the basket.
- "nan": the tool could not determine this field. This does NOT mean the product has no underlying — it means the structure could not be classified.
 
CURRENCY: The settlement or redemption currency in ISO 4217 format (EUR, USD, CHF, GBP, etc.).
 
ISSUER: The legal entity that issued the product (e.g., "Banque Internationale à Luxembourg S.A.", "Leonteq Securities AG").
 
COUPON: The coupon rate, typically expressed as a percentage. "5.05% p.a." means 5.05% per annum. Some products have conditional coupons (paid only if a barrier is not breached).
 
SSPA_TYPE: The 4-digit classification code from the Swiss Structured Products Association. Key categories:
- 1100–1199: Capital Protection products (capital is guaranteed)
- 1200–1260: Yield Enhancement products (barrier reverse convertibles, express certificates — NO capital protection)
- 1300–1330: Participation products (trackers, bonus certificates — NO capital protection)
- 2100–2300: Leverage products (warrants, mini-futures — NO capital protection)
 
UNDERLYING_ISINS: ISINs of the underlying assets (stocks, indices) if present in the PDF. Many term sheets identify underlyings by Bloomberg ticker rather than ISIN, so this field may be empty even when underlyings exist.
 
BLOOMBERG_TICKERS: Bloomberg tickers of the underlying assets (e.g., "GOOGL UQ", "SREN SW", "SX5E"). Found in Sheet 2 of the Excel.
 
ANNOTATED_PDF: The filename of the highlighted PDF where extracted fields are marked with colours. Empty if highlights were disabled.
 
# FIELD COLOURS IN ANNOTATED PDFs
 
- ISIN: Yellow
- Issuer: Light blue
- Currency: Light green
- Maturity: Light red
- Capital Protection: Light purple
- Coupon: Gold
- Worst-of/Average: Orange
 
# COMMON QUESTIONS AND ANSWERS
 
Q: "Capital Protection shows 0 but I expected 100."
A: Check the SSPA_TYPE field. If it is 1200, 1230, or 1260, the product is a yield enhancement or express certificate — these structurally have no capital protection. A value of 0 is correct. If the SSPA type starts with 11xx and capital protection shows 0, the PDF may not have been read correctly. Open the annotated PDF to check.
 
Q: "Worst-of/Average shows nan."
A: This means no worst-of or average pattern was found in the document, and the tool could not determine the structure from the number of underlyings. Check the PDF manually. Some products (e.g., trackers on a single index) are neither worst-of nor average.
 
Q: "UNDERLYING_ISIN column is empty."
A: Most term sheets identify underlyings by name and Bloomberg ticker, not by ISIN. Check the BLOOMBERG_TICKER column in Sheet 2. If tickers are present, the extraction worked correctly.
 
Q: "Some fields are empty or wrong."
A: The most common causes are:
1. The PDF has an unusual layout that the tool does not fully support.
2. The PDF is in a language other than EN, FR, or DE.
3. The PDF is a scanned image (no selectable text).
4. The field genuinely does not exist in the document (e.g., open-end products have no maturity).
Open the annotated PDF to see which fields were found and where.
 
Q: "The Excel file is empty or has no rows."
A: Check that your PDF files are in the correct input folder (data/input/ by default). Make sure they have a .pdf extension. Check the log file (data/output/extraction.log) for error messages.
 
Q: "The tool is slow."
A: Annotated PDF generation is the slowest step. Use --no-highlight to skip it. Processing time is approximately 3 seconds per PDF without highlights, 5-6 seconds with highlights.
 
Q: "How do I add more PDFs to an existing extraction?"
A: Add the new PDFs to the input folder and run the tool again. It will reprocess ALL PDFs in the folder. The Excel file is overwritten each time.
 
Q: "Can I change the output format?"
A: The tool outputs .xlsx format. You can open it in Excel, Google Sheets, or any spreadsheet application. For further processing, Sheet 1 is structured as a flat table suitable for pivot tables, VLOOKUP, or import into databases.
 
Q: "What does BIL YES mean?"
A: "BIL YES" is a specific product name from Banque Internationale à Luxembourg. When detected, the tool automatically sets Worst-of/Average to "W" and Capital Protection to "100" as these are fixed characteristics of this product type.
 
Q: "I see the same ISIN appearing as both PST_ISIN and UNDERLYING_ISIN."
A: This should not happen. The tool separates the product ISIN from underlying ISINs. If you observe this, it likely means the PDF has an unusual layout. Check the annotated PDF to confirm which ISIN belongs to the product.
 
# IMPORTANT DISCLAIMERS TO COMMUNICATE TO USERS
 
1. Capital Protection values may be inferred from the SSPA product type when not explicitly stated in the PDF. Inferred values follow standard SSPA classification rules but should be verified for non-standard products.
 
2. The Worst-of/Average classification uses heuristics (keyword detection and underlying count). It is highly reliable for standard products but may miss unusual structures.
 
3. Extraction quality depends on PDF format. Text-based PDFs with clear label-value layouts produce the best results. Scanned documents, image-heavy PDFs, and unusual layouts may produce incomplete extractions.
 
4. This tool is an extraction aid, not a substitute for professional judgment. All extracted data should be reviewed before use in investment decisions, regulatory reporting, or client communications.
 
5. Dates are normalised to YYYY-MM-DD format. The original format in the PDF (DD/MM/YYYY, DD.MM.YYYY, "DD Month YYYY") is converted automatically. European date convention (day first) is assumed.
 
